1.  Purpose
The purpose of this document is to establish a set of practical, actionable guidelines for secure development using Google Apps Script. It is intended to help development teams — including non-technical users leveraging AI tools to generate scripts — design, build, and maintain Apps Script projects that uphold best practices in security, especially when handling sensitive data, integrating with external APIs, or deploying scripts in shared environments.
By aligning teams on secure development standards, this guide aims to minimize the risk of data breaches, unauthorized access, privilege escalation, and other common security pitfalls in Google Workspace automation and extensions.
NEW IN v2.0:  Version 2.0 adds six new sections covering: Script Execution Model, Trigger Security, Third-Party Libraries, AI-Assisted Development Security, Logging Best Practices, and Input Validation — reflecting the evolving threat landscape and the increasing use of AI tools to generate Apps Script code.
2.  Scope
This document applies to all engineers, developers, and technical stakeholders involved in the development, deployment, or maintenance of Google Apps Script solutions across the organization.
It also applies to non-technical employees who use AI tools (such as Google Gemini, Claude, or GitHub Copilot) to generate Apps Script code for deployment — even partially. The use of an AI tool to write code does not transfer security responsibility away from the person deploying it.
These guidelines apply to both standalone scripts and scripts bound to Google Workspace documents (Sheets, Docs, Forms, Slides, etc.).
3.  Why Google Apps Script Security Matters
NEW IN v2.0:  This section has been updated in v2.0 to include execution identity risks and AI-generated code risks — two attack vectors not present in v1.4.
Google Apps Script projects often deal with sensitive data (e.g., emails, financial records, employee information) and privileged APIs (e.g., Gmail metadata, Drive files, user profiles). Without proper security controls, these scripts can:
Expose internal data to unauthorized users
Be exploited for lateral movement within a compromised Workspace environment
Silently run with elevated credentials via 'Execute as Me' — giving any triggering user the deployer's full permissions without their knowledge
Transmit internal data to external services via unreviewed UrlFetchApp calls
Accumulate OAuth permissions that are never audited or revoked

The AI-Generated Code Risk
As AI tools become commonly used for generating Apps Script code, a new category of risk has emerged. AI models optimize for code that works — not for code that is secure. Common issues observed in AI-generated Apps Script include:
Overly broad OAuth scopes: Overly broad OAuth scopes: AI tools routinely request full Drive, Gmail, or Spreadsheets access when only a narrow scope is needed.
Hard-coded credentials: Hard-coded credentials: AI-generated code frequently contains placeholder API keys, example tokens, or sample credentials that resemble real values.
Insecure logging: Insecure logging: AI models often log full API request and response objects for debugging — which may contain secrets or PII.
Default 'Execute as Me': Default 'Execute as Me': AI-suggested deployment configurations almost always choose the deployer's credentials and broad access levels to avoid runtime errors.
Unvetted library suggestions: Unvetted library suggestions: AI tools may suggest third-party libraries by Script ID without any security vetting.

WARNING:  Deploying AI-generated Apps Script code without a human security review is a policy violation, regardless of whether the code 'works' in testing. Functional code is not the same as secure code.
4.  How to Store Secrets in Google Apps Script?
Securing secrets such as API keys, tokens, and passwords is critical in any software system. If these secrets are leaked, they can expose your systems to data breaches, financial loss, and unauthorized access.
This is particularly important in Google Apps Script, where scripts often access external services and are linked to shared documents. A poorly protected secret can be exposed to anyone with access to the script or document.
The following methods are ranked from least secure to most robust:
Secrets in Plain Text
Encrypted Secrets in Code
Script Properties
User Properties
Detached Script
Do Not Store Secrets (Prompt Every Time)
Google Cloud Secret Manager  [RECOMMENDED — NEW in v2.0]

AI-SPECIFIC:  If an AI tool generated any part of your script, search every string literal in the generated code for placeholder credentials, sample tokens, and hard-coded values. AI models frequently insert example API keys that resemble real values. Strings matching patterns like 'AIza', 'sk-', 'Bearer', 'token=', or base64 blobs longer than 20 characters should be treated as suspect until verified.
5.  PropertiesService
Allows scripts to store simple data in key-value pairs scoped to one script, one user of a script, or one document in which an add-on is used. Properties cannot be shared between scripts.
This class has three methods:
getScriptProperties() getScriptProperties() — Gets a property store that all users can access, but only within this script.
getUserProperties() getUserProperties() — Gets a property store that only the current user can access, and only within this script.
getDocumentProperties() getDocumentProperties() — Gets a property store that all users can access within the open document, spreadsheet, or form.

WARNING:  Anyone with edit access to a script can read and modify its Script Properties. This means Script Properties are only as secure as your edit access list. Treat everyone with edit access as someone who can read every stored secret.
For detailed implementation guidance, refer to the official Google documentation:
PropertiesService reference: https://developers.google.com/apps-script/reference/properties/properties-service
Saving, reading, modifying, and deleting data: https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/properties#saving_data
Managing properties manually: https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/properties#manage_script_properties_manually
Storage Methods
1.  Secrets in Plain Text
Pros: Pros: Quick and simple.
Cons: Cons: No security. Anyone with read permissions to the script or the document it is attached to can access the secret.
WARNING:  Never use plain text secrets in any script that will be shared, run by others, or connected to production systems. This method is prohibited under AppSec policy.
2.  Encrypted Secrets in Code
You can encrypt secrets using a library like crypto-js, then prompt the user for a password to decrypt them.
Pros: Pros: More secure than plain text storage.
Cons: Cons: The script must decrypt the secret at runtime where it can be logged or otherwise compromised by anyone with edit permissions. Encrypted secrets are accessible to anyone with read permissions; an attacker could save the encrypted value and brute-force the password offline. Cannot schedule the script to run automatically. Prompting users for passwords encourages insecure password reuse.
Overall, this method is complex but rarely worth the complexity given better alternatives.
3.  Script Properties
Google Apps Script's PropertiesService allows you to store values attached to a script. Script Properties are visible and manageable in the project settings.
Pros: Pros: Only script editors can access the stored secrets.
Cons: Cons: All script editors can access the secrets. If the script is bound to a document, all document editors are also script editors.
This is a reasonable option if edit access is tightly controlled and all editors are trusted.
4.  User Properties
Similar to Script Properties, but accessible only to the user who created them.
Important exception: Important exception: User properties of the owner of a Google Sheets document are accessible to any user via a custom function. This is an obscurely documented edge case that can be exploited.
Pros: Pros: Only you can access the secret unless the script logs it at runtime.
Cons: Cons: If other users can edit the script, they can log the secret when you run the script. If other people need to run the script independently, you must share the secrets with them.
5.  Detached Script
A completely separate script project, not attached to any document, with limited edit access.
Pros: Pros: One of the most secure options — only people with edit access to the detached script can compromise the secrets.
Cons: Cons: Requires broad permission scopes (e.g., full spreadsheets access) to update a document from outside. Cannot manipulate the document's UI (alerts, menus, etc.). Relies mostly on time-scheduled triggers; triggering from a document requires a more complex setup.
6.  Do Not Store Secrets (Prompt Every Time)
The safest way to handle secrets is to not store them at all — prompt users to enter them at runtime.
Pros: Pros: Secrets are never stored anywhere.
Cons: Cons: Cannot use time-based triggers. Vulnerable to script edits. Repetitive and error-prone. May encourage insecure secret reuse.
Best suited to sensitive workflows where storing secrets is explicitly prohibited by policy.
7.  Google Cloud Secret Manager  [RECOMMENDED]
NEW IN v2.0:  Google Cloud Secret Manager is the recommended approach for production scripts in v2.0. It provides centralized secret management, access audit logs, version control, and automatic rotation — none of which are available with PropertiesService.
Google Cloud Secret Manager stores secrets as versioned, encrypted objects in GCP, accessible to Apps Script via the Secret Manager API.
Pros: Pros: Secrets are stored outside the script entirely. Access is controlled via GCP IAM roles (not script edit access). Every access is logged in Cloud Audit Logs. Supports secret versioning and rotation without code changes.
Cons: Cons: Requires a GCP project and Secret Manager API enablement. More setup than Script Properties. Requires the script.external_request scope to call the API.
To access a secret from Apps Script:
function getSecret(secretName) {
  const projectId = 'your-gcp-project-id';
  const url = `https://secretmanager.googleapis.com/v1/projects/${projectId}/secrets/${secretName}/versions/latest:access`;
  const token = ScriptApp.getOAuthToken();
  const response = UrlFetchApp.fetch(url, {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }
  });
  const data = JSON.parse(response.getContentText());
  return Utilities.newBlob(Utilities.base64Decode(data.payload.data)).getDataAsString();
}
BEST PRACTICE:  For production scripts handling API keys, OAuth tokens, or any credentials used in automated/trigger-based execution, Secret Manager is the required approach. Script Properties are acceptable for low-sensitivity, manually-run scripts with tightly controlled edit access.
6.  Choosing the Right Storage Method
When choosing how to store secrets in Apps Script, evaluate the following factors:
Access and Trust: Access and Trust — Who has access to this script? Avoid Script Properties if the script is attached to a broadly shared document. Edit access = secret access.
Script Execution Type: Script Execution Type — Does it run on a schedule or manually? Scheduled (trigger-based) scripts cannot use 'prompt every time.' Prefer Secret Manager or Script Properties with restricted edit access.
User Interaction: User Interaction — Does the script run for multiple users or just you? Per-user secrets require User Properties. Shared secrets require Script Properties or Secret Manager.
Risk Level: Risk Level — What does the secret grant access to? The more damaging a leaked secret, the stronger the storage method required. Production API keys with write access require Secret Manager.
AI-Generated Scripts: AI-Generated Scripts — Was this script generated by an AI tool? Inspect all string literals for placeholder credentials before choosing a storage method.

Storage Method
When to Use
Secret Manager
Production scripts, trigger-based, any script with API keys or tokens granting write/admin access
Script Properties
Low-sensitivity secrets, manually-run scripts, tightly controlled edit access (3 or fewer trusted editors)
User Properties
Secrets that belong to a single user; not appropriate when multiple users run the script independently
Detached Script
Advanced use cases where secret isolation from a shared document is required
Runtime Prompt
Sensitive workflows where storing secrets is prohibited; not compatible with trigger-based automation
Plain Text
NEVER — prohibited in all production and shared scripts

7.  Understanding and Managing Authorization Scopes in Google Apps Script
Authorization scopes define what your script can access or do on your behalf. The first time you run a script, Google prompts you to grant it the necessary permissions. Sometimes these prompts include warnings about unverified apps or potential security risks — do not ignore these warnings.
8.  Why Authorization Scopes Matter
Authorization scopes define the boundaries of what your script is allowed to do with your Google account and data. Understanding and managing these scopes is critical for maintaining security.
Consider the difference between two similar-sounding scopes:
Broad Scope: https://www.googleapis.com/auth/spreadsheets — This allows the script full access to all spreadsheets you can access — read, write, delete — essentially acting as you.
Restricted Scope: https://www.googleapis.com/auth/spreadsheets.currentonly — This restricts access to only the spreadsheet the script is bound to, significantly reducing potential damage in case of misuse.
A shared script with elevated scopes can be silently altered by another user and executed under your account — potentially exfiltrating data or altering documents without your knowledge. Once permissions are granted, reviewing or revoking them is not always straightforward.
9.  How Apps Script Determines Scopes
There are three main ways scopes are defined in an Apps Script project:
1.  Automatic (Implicit) Scopes
Apps Script infers required scopes based on the services your code uses (e.g., SpreadsheetApp, DriveApp, UrlFetchApp). This is convenient but often overly permissive — using SpreadsheetApp without scope annotation can trigger a request for full access to all spreadsheets, even if the script only needs access to the current one.
2.  Manual (Explicit) Scopes via appsscript.json
To take control over which scopes are requested, define them manually in the appsscript.json manifest file:
Enable manifest editing: go to Project Settings and enable 'Show appsscript.json manifest file in editor.'
Add the oauthScopes field with the specific scopes required. Example:
{
  "timeZone": "America/New_York",
  "dependencies": {},
  "exceptionLogging": "STACKDRIVER",
  "runtimeVersion": "V8",
  "oauthScopes": [
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/spreadsheets.currentonly",
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/script.external_request"
  ]
}
WARNING:  When setting scopes manually, you must include all required scopes — missing any will cause runtime errors. However, it is better to encounter a runtime error from a missing scope than to silently over-permission the script.
3.  JSDoc Annotations (@OnlyCurrentDoc)
Placing the @OnlyCurrentDoc annotation at the top of your script forces Apps Script to request only 'current document' access scopes for bound services. This is a middle ground between convenience and security — appropriate for most common use cases.
/**
 * @OnlyCurrentDoc
 */
function myFunction() {
  // Script can only access the current document
}
