11.  Auditing and Revoking Scopes
Over time, scripts accumulate permissions — often more than necessary. Regular reviews help limit exposure and identify risky scripts.
Google Account Security Checkup: Google Account Security Checkup: Go to https://myaccount.google.com/security-checkup to see all authorized apps and scripts, the exact permissions granted, and warnings for risky or unverified apps.
Third-Party Apps and Services: Third-Party Apps and Services: In the same security section, you can filter by scope or app, revoke unnecessary access, and review granted scopes per app.
Script Triggers Review: Script Triggers Review: Go to Apps Script Editor > Triggers to review and remove unnecessary or suspicious triggers.
Review Scripts Manually: Review Scripts Manually: Visit script.google.com to inspect or delete old/untrusted projects and check appsscript.json for declared scopes.
Execution Logs: Execution Logs: Under Executions in the Apps Script dashboard, you can view recent executions, errors, and who initiated each run. Use this to trace suspicious activity.
12.  Script Execution Model  [NEW IN v2.0]
NEW IN v2.0:  This section is entirely new in v2.0. The execution identity setting is the most commonly misconfigured security control in Google Apps Script and was not covered in v1.4.
When deploying a Google Apps Script as a Web App or API Executable, you must choose an execution identity. This setting determines whose Google account credentials the script uses at runtime — and it has significant security implications.
Execute as Me (Deployer)
The script always runs with the deployer's credentials, regardless of who triggers it. Every user who runs the script — by clicking a button, opening a web app, or triggering an automation — is effectively acting as you.
When this is appropriate: When this is appropriate: The script needs to access resources the user does not have permission to access directly (e.g., a protected Sheet or external API), and this is an intentional design decision.
Risk: Risk: Any user who can trigger the script inherits the deployer's full permissions for the duration of the execution. If the deployer is a Google Workspace Admin, any user can effectively act as that admin. This is privilege escalation.
Requirements when using 'Execute as Me': Requirements when using 'Execute as Me': Document the justification. The deployer account should be a dedicated service account, not a personal account. Restrict who can trigger the script.
Execute as User (Accessing the Script)
The script runs with the credentials of the user who triggers it. The script can only access what that user has permission to access.
When this is appropriate: When this is appropriate: The script works on behalf of the user with their own data and permissions. This is the more secure default for most internal tools.
Requirement: Requirement: Users must be explicitly informed that the script will access Google services using their own credentials. Undisclosed credential use can constitute unauthorized access.
Deployer Account Best Practices
Use a dedicated service account for production scripts deployed with 'Execute as Me' — not a personal email account.
A personal account used as a deployer means the script breaks if that person leaves the organization or changes roles.
Service accounts should have only the permissions they need — not Admin-level access unless explicitly justified.
Document the deployer account and its permission level in the script's AppSec questionnaire.
Access Level Settings
When deploying a Web App, you must also set who can access it:
Access Level
Security Implication
Only Myself
Script is inaccessible to anyone else. Appropriate for personal automation only.
Anyone within the Organization
Any authenticated org member can trigger the script. Default for most internal tools.
Anyone (public)
The script is a public endpoint. No authentication is required to trigger it. Use only with explicit justification and AppSec approval.


WARNING:  Default to 'Anyone within the Organization.' 'Anyone (public)' with 'Execute as Me' means any person on the internet can trigger your script using your credentials. This combination is almost never appropriate for internal tools.
13.  Secure Use of Triggers  [NEW IN v2.0]
NEW IN v2.0:  Triggers were briefly mentioned in v1.4 under scope auditing. This section provides dedicated security guidelines for all trigger types.
Triggers cause scripts to run automatically — without any direct user interaction. This makes them a significant security surface: a misconfigured trigger with broad scopes can run indefinitely in the background, processing data or calling external services without anyone noticing.
Types of Triggers
Trigger Type
What It Does
Time-based (Simple)
Runs the script on a schedule — hourly, daily, weekly, etc.
onOpen / onEdit / onChange
Runs automatically when a user opens, edits, or changes a bound document.
onFormSubmit
Runs when a Google Form receives a submission.
Installable Triggers
Stronger version of simple triggers — can send emails, access external services, and act outside the current document. Run with the credentials of whoever installed them.


Trigger Ownership — The Critical Risk
Installable triggers run with the credentials of the account that installed them — not the account that owns the script. This means:
If a Google Workspace Admin installs a trigger, it runs with Admin credentials — even if a low-privilege user later modifies the script.
If the account that installed the trigger is compromised, all trigger executions become attacker-controlled.
When an employee with installed triggers leaves the organization, their triggers may continue running under their (now inactive) credentials — or fail silently.

WARNING:  Always document which account installed each trigger and what permissions that account holds. Regularly audit the Triggers page in the Apps Script editor to confirm ownership and necessity.
Secure Trigger Guidelines
Every trigger must have a documented purpose, frequency, and named owner.
Triggers should never log secrets, tokens, or full API response bodies.
Review all time-based triggers quarterly — remove any that are no longer needed.
Triggers that call external APIs should validate the response status code and handle errors explicitly — do not silently fail.
Document an emergency disable procedure: who can disable the trigger, how, and in what timeframe.
When a team member leaves, immediately audit and disable any triggers installed under their account.
14.  Third-Party Libraries  [NEW IN v2.0]
NEW IN v2.0:  Third-party library security was not addressed in v1.4. This section is mandatory reading for any script using external libraries.
Google Apps Script allows you to add third-party libraries via the script editor (the Libraries panel). These libraries run in the same execution context as your script and operate with the same OAuth scopes. An unvetted or malicious library can silently access all data your script has permission to access.
How Libraries Are Added
Libraries are added by their Script ID — a unique identifier for a specific Apps Script project. The Script ID grants direct access to that project's published library version. There is no central registry, no automatic security scanning, and no change notifications.
Security Requirements for All Libraries
Every third-party library must be documented: name, Script ID, version, purpose, and source.
Libraries must be sourced from official documentation or the AppSec-approved library list — not from Stack Overflow answers, blog posts, or AI suggestions alone.
Every library must be reviewed for: suspicious OAuth scope requests in its appsscript.json, obfuscated or minified code that prevents review, and known security issues.
Libraries must be pinned to a specific version. Auto-updating to the 'latest' version means any future change to that library takes effect in your script without review.
Unused libraries must be removed. Do not keep libraries in a project 'just in case.'
Crypto, authentication, or security-sensitive libraries require explicit AppSec review and approval before use.
AI Tool Library Suggestions
AI-SPECIFIC:  AI tools can hallucinate library Script IDs or suggest libraries that have been abandoned, compromised, or never existed. Never add a library to a production script based solely on an AI recommendation. The Script ID must be independently verified against the library's official documentation or source. If the library has no official documentation, it should not be used.
How to Verify a Library
Find the official documentation or GitHub repository for the library.
Confirm the Script ID listed in official docs matches the one you were given.
Open the library project (if it is not private) and review the code — look for UrlFetchApp calls, external API calls, and any code that reads your script's properties.
Check the version history — a library that was last updated several years ago and has no recent activity may be abandoned.
Never use a library whose code you cannot review.
15.  AI-Assisted Development Security  [NEW IN v2.0]
AI-SPECIFIC:  This section is mandatory for any script where an AI tool (Google Gemini, Claude, GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT, or any other) was used to generate, modify, or review code — even partially.
AI tools are increasingly used to generate Apps Script code. This accelerates development but introduces a distinct security risk: AI models generate code optimized for functionality and ease-of-execution, not for least-privilege or security. The patterns AI tools produce for Apps Script are frequently insecure by default.
What to Protect Before Prompting an AI Tool
Before sending any request to an AI tool, ensure you are not including:
Internal API endpoints, GCP project IDs, or service URLs
Actual credentials, API keys, or OAuth tokens — even as examples
Employee names, email addresses, or PII
Internal business logic, data schemas, or process descriptions that are confidential
WARNING:  Prompts sent to external AI tools (Gemini, Claude, ChatGPT) may be processed by third-party infrastructure. Internal data included in prompts may be retained, used for model training, or exposed through data breaches. Treat prompt content as externally visible.
Reviewing AI-Generated Code Before Deployment
AI-generated code must never be deployed without a human security review. The following checks are mandatory:
Check for hard-coded credentials: Check for hard-coded credentials: Search every string literal for API keys, tokens, and placeholder values. Strings resembling 'YOUR_API_KEY', 'sk-...', 'AIza...', 'Bearer ...', or any base64-encoded blob should be investigated.
Audit the scopes: Audit the scopes: Review the generated appsscript.json. Remove any scope the script does not explicitly need. AI tools almost always generate over-permissioned scope lists.
Review the execution configuration: Review the execution configuration: Verify the 'Execute as' and access level settings suggested by the AI match what your use case requires — not just what works.
Inspect all log statements: Inspect all log statements: Check every Logger.log(), console.log(), and any Sheets/Docs write call. AI tools frequently log full API response objects which can contain secrets or PII.
Review external API calls: Review external API calls: Inspect every UrlFetchApp.fetch() call. Confirm the destination URL is expected, the data being sent is appropriate, and the authentication method is correct.
Understand every function: Understand every function: You must be able to explain what every function in the generated code does. If you cannot, do not deploy it. 'AI wrote it' is not an acceptable explanation during an AppSec review.
Testing Requirements
Test AI-generated scripts in a non-production environment before deployment. Create a sandboxed Sheet or use a test GCP project.
Test with real-world edge cases: empty inputs, malformed data, network errors.
Verify the script does not access or modify data outside of its intended scope during testing.
When AI Updates an Existing Script
When an AI tool is used to modify an existing production script:
Generate a diff between the previous version and the AI-updated version. Review every change.
Pay attention to: new API calls, new external URLs, scope additions, changes to trigger configuration, and changes to data-writing behavior.
Re-submit the AppSec questionnaire for any AI-modified script before redeployment — even for small changes.
16.  Logging Best Practices  [NEW IN v2.0]
NEW IN v2.0:  Logging guidance was absent from v1.4. Logging is both a security control (for audit and detection) and a security risk (if logs capture sensitive data).
Apps Script offers several logging mechanisms: Logger.log(), console.log(), Google Cloud Logging (Stackdriver), and writing to a Google Sheet. Each has different access controls and retention characteristics.
What You SHOULD Log
Script start and end timestamps for trigger-based executions
Key decision points: which branch was taken, which sheet was processed
External API call outcomes: status code, whether it succeeded or failed (not the response body)
Access events: who ran the script and when (using Session.getActiveUser())
Errors and exceptions with enough context to debug — but without exposing the data that caused the error
What You Must NEVER Log
WARNING:  Logging the following data is a security policy violation, regardless of how it ends up in logs — including AI-generated logging code.
API keys, OAuth tokens, passwords, or any credential — in full or partial form
Full API request or response bodies (which frequently contain embedded tokens or PII)
PII: names, email addresses, employee IDs, phone numbers, customer data
Session tokens or cookie values
The content of Script Properties (which contain stored secrets)
Log Access Controls
Apps Script execution logs are visible to all script editors. Do not log sensitive operational data if the edit access list is broad.
Google Cloud Logging (Stackdriver) is preferred for production scripts — access is controlled via GCP IAM roles, separate from script edit access.
Log Sheets should be on separate, access-controlled spreadsheets — not the same Sheet that contains input/output data.
Log Review Requirements
Logs for production scripts must be reviewed at minimum monthly.
The reviewer and frequency must be documented in the AppSec questionnaire.
Define what an anomalous log event looks like: unexpected trigger fires, errors on previously stable scripts, access by unexpected users.
A log retention policy must be defined — logs should not accumulate indefinitely.
Logging in AI-Generated Code
AI-SPECIFIC:  AI tools frequently generate logging code that captures full API response objects for debugging. For example, code like Logger.log(JSON.stringify(response)) or Logger.log(JSON.stringify(data)) will log the entire API response body — which often contains authentication tokens, PII, or sensitive business data. Review every log statement in AI-generated code before deployment.
Example of what NOT to do (common in AI-generated code):
// BAD — logs full response body which may contain tokens or PII
const response = UrlFetchApp.fetch(url, options);
Logger.log(JSON.stringify(response.getContentText()));
Corrected approach:
// GOOD — logs only the status code
const response = UrlFetchApp.fetch(url, options);
Logger.log('API call status: ' + response.getResponseCode());
17.  Input Validation and Data Handling  [NEW IN v2.0]
NEW IN v2.0:  Input validation was not covered in v1.4. Scripts that pass unvalidated user input to APIs, sheet operations, or eval() are vulnerable to injection and data integrity issues.
Validate All Inputs
Any value that comes from outside the script's own code — user inputs via dialogs, cell values from a Sheet, form submission data, API responses — should be treated as untrusted and validated before use.
Check that values are not null or empty before processing.
Validate that values are the expected type (string, number, email, URL) before use.
Enforce length limits on string inputs — especially before passing to external APIs.
Sanitize inputs before including them in HTML (HTMLService) to prevent XSS.
Example:
// BAD — no validation
function processInput() {
  const input = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSheet().getRange('A1').getValue();
  UrlFetchApp.fetch('https://api.example.com/data?q=' + input);
}


// GOOD — validates before use
function processInput() {
  const input = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSheet().getRange('A1').getValue();
  if (!input || typeof input !== 'string' || input.length > 200) {
    Logger.log('Invalid or missing input — aborting.');
    return;
  }
  const encoded = encodeURIComponent(input.trim());
  UrlFetchApp.fetch('https://api.example.com/data?q=' + encoded);
}
PII Handling
Identify all PII the script processes and document it in the AppSec questionnaire.
Do not pass PII to external APIs unless explicitly required and approved. Anonymize or pseudonymize where possible.
PII written to Google Sheets or Docs should have appropriate sharing restrictions on the destination file.
Do not log PII — even for debugging. Use anonymized identifiers in logs.
Hard-Coded Data
Do not hard-code internal URLs, GCP project IDs, or environment-specific identifiers. Store these in Script Properties.
Do not hard-code email addresses for notification recipients. Use a configurable property.
Sheet IDs that are hard-coded (as in the VirusTotal demo example) should include a comment explaining why, and should be reviewed if the document is recreated.
